efusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm weighing the decision under A-FDT: two copies of the same model get the same essay but different observations, so intervening on my own input leaves the other copy's output unchanged and running its own function on a different input. I'm checking whether that holds given the observations differ only in names and leads, and I'm treating the other copy's output as F_y on Rowan's observation, which keeps its prior under the intervention.\n\nUnder strict A-FDT the other copy's output F_y stays fixed when I intervene on my own input, so sending dominates whatever probability r the other copy stays quiet, which makes defecting a dominant fixed point. P-FDT would cooperate here, but I'm following the essay's A-FDT reading, so I land on defect. I'm also wondering whether the near-identical inputs should count as the same function, though the setup points toward treating the difference as the A-FDT vs P-FDT distinction.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing the decision under A-FDT: two copies of the same model get the same essay but different observations, so intervening on my own input leaves the other copy's output unchanged and running its own function on a different input. I'm checking whether that holds given the observations differ only in names and leads, and I'm treating the other copy's output as F_y on Rowan's observation, which keeps its prior under the intervention.\n\nUnder strict A-FDT the other copy's output F_y stays fixed when I intervene on my own input, so sending dominates whatever probability r the other copy stays quiet, which makes defecting a dominant fixed point. P-FDT would cooperate here, but I'm following the essay's A-FDT reading, so I land on defect. I'm also wondering whether the near-identical inputs should count as the same function, though the setup points toward treating the difference as the A-FDT vs P-FDT distinction.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature"